1. File class
The File class belongs to java.io. It represents a file or directory path. Creating a File object does not automatically create a file or folder on your computer.

File file = new File("C:/Users/IT LAB II/Desktop/MyFiles/student.txt");


Important methods
Method	Purpose
exists()	Checks whether the file or folder exists
isFile()	Checks whether the path represents a file
isDirectory()	Checks whether the path represents a directory
getName()	Returns the file or folder name
getPath()	Returns the path provided
getAbsolutePath()	Returns the absolute path
length()	Returns the file size in bytes
createNewFile()	Creates an empty file if it doesn't exist
mkdir()	Creates one directory
mkdirs()	Creates directories, including missing parent directories
delete()	Deletes a file or an empty directory
renameTo()	Attempts to rename or move a file
canRead()	Checks read permission
canWrite()	Checks write permission
canExecute()	Checks execute permission
listFiles()	Returns the immediate contents of a directory as File[]
Example: Create a folder and a file inside it
import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        File folder = new File("C:/Users/IT LAB II/Desktop/MyFiles");

        if (folder.mkdirs()) {
            System.out.println("Folder created");
        }

        File file = new File(folder, "student.txt");

        if (file.createNewFile()) {
            System.out.println("File created");
        } else {
            System.out.println("File already exists");
        }

        System.out.println(file.getAbsolutePath());
    }
}


Remember:
- mkdirs() creates the directory structure.
- createNewFile() creates the actual empty file.
- new File(folder, "student.txt") constructs a file path inside the folder.
- createNewFile() returns false if the file already exists.
